# Cube Core — Software Architecture

Canonical docs: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/ROLES-AND-TENANCY.md`, `docs/TECH-STACK.md`, `docs/AUTH-2FA.md`.

When building any feature, **read those docs + existing code first**. Do not invent a parallel architecture.

## Four apps (do not merge)

| App | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `cube-core-server` | System of record: GraphQL, Prisma, auth, domain, audit |
| `cube-core-admin` | Main console UI (SuperAdmin). BFF + Apollo → server |
| `cube-core-tenant` | Sub-tenant console UI (Admin/Editor). BFF + Apollo → server |
| `cube-core-website` | Public marketing site; public GraphQL reads only |

- Domain logic lives in **server** `src/modules/*` services.
- Resolvers stay thin — call services, never put business rules only in UI.
- Frontend GraphQL documents live in `src/graphql/*.gql`; generated Apollo artifacts live in `src/lib/apollo/generated`. Website operations are public reads only. Never localStorage tokens.

## Locked stack (do not swap)

- Server: Node HTTP + TypeScript + Zod + PostgreSQL + Prisma 6 + GraphQL Yoga + SDL (`src/graphql/schema.graphql`), jose cookies, Argon2id/bcrypt verify
- Frontends: Next.js App Router + Tailwind + **shadcn/ui** + Apollo Client and GraphQL Code Generator
- No Nest, tRPC, Redux, alternate UI kits, or microservices for v1

## Tenancy & roles (hard)

```text
MAIN tenant (slug: core-super-admin, singleton)
  └── users + tenant_memberships → SUPERADMIN only

SUB tenants (children of MAIN)
  └── users + tenant_memberships → ADMIN | EDITOR
```

- Unified identity: `users`, `roles`, `tenant_memberships` (not MainUser/SubUser).
- **Never** create a second MAIN tenant.
- **Never** let SuperAdmin obtain a sub-tenant / tenant-app session.
- **Never** let Admin/Editor sign into the main console.
- Separate cookies/audiences: `ADMIN_CONSOLE` vs `TENANT_APP`.
- UI may hide actions; **server must enforce** role + tenant on every operation.

## Auth pattern (reuse, do not duplicate)

1. Password (username **or** email) → short-lived challenge (no protected session)
2. Activation: username + password + mandatory TOTP + recovery codes (shown once)
3. Returning login: TOTP verify (recovery-code path available)
4. Then issue console-specific session

Password reset: **SuperAdmin-generated links only** (no public forgot-password).

## Server layout

```text
src/index.ts                                    # Node HTTP + Yoga → /graphql only
src/modules/{auth,tenants,users,dashboard}/     # domain services
src/graphql/schema.graphql                      # single SDL
src/graphql/resolvers/{auth,dashboard,tenants,users}/  # one op per *-resolver.ts
src/lib/{db,auth,errors}
prisma/schema.prisma
```

- Server is **GraphQL-only** — no Next.js `app/`, no REST `/auth` or `/api`.
- One SDL; resolvers split by domain folder (not one giant `resolvers.ts`). Business logic stays in `src/modules/*`.
- Validate inputs with Zod in services.
- Write `audit_logs` on privileged mutations.
- Prefer unified `users` / memberships / sessions over inventing parallel auth tables.

## Frontend layout

- Admin SuperAdmin nav: Dashboard, Tenants, **Users** (`/users` = Main Tenant User Management), Settings, Audit, Health
- Admin User Management manages **MAIN SUPERADMIN memberships only**
- Sub-tenant user invites: activation links (`createAdminUser` / tenant Users page)
- Tenant app: strictly one sub-tenant scope; reject SuperAdmin at login
- Run `npm run graphql:codegen` in each frontend after SDL or operation document changes
- Website GraphQL is public-read only; never add privileged mutations
- Tenant domains map normalized unique MANAGEMENT / WEBSITE hostnames to the stable tenant UUID. Only verified `ACTIVE` mappings route production traffic; saved domains remain pending until ownership and hosting/HTTPS setup are complete. `tenantByHostname` is public identity resolution only; never use hostname as authentication or authorization. Local development uses one server-managed `localhost` assignment, stored separately from production domains; it selects the tenant for shared ports 3001 and 3002 and must never be supplied by a URL parameter or environment UUID. Trust forwarded host headers only from explicitly trusted infrastructure.
- Admin / tenant / website use Next.js `src/` layout (`src/app`, `src/components`, `src/lib`, …)
- Admin/tenant/website GraphQL docs: `src/graphql/*.gql` + `src/lib/apollo`; run `npm run graphql:codegen` after server SDL changes

## Feature checklist (before claiming done)

1. Correct app(s) touched; server owns authz + data rules
2. Role/tenant isolation unchanged
3. SDL + resolvers + service (not UI-only filtering)
4. Docs updated if behavior/architecture/roles changed
5. Reused patterns; no alternate stack

## GraphQL field selection and efficient reads

- GraphQL operation documents request only fields their screen/action uses; avoid broad “just in case” selections.
- GraphQL limits response fields, but does not automatically limit database reads. Use explicit Prisma `select` projections in read services; avoid full-model `include: true` on list paths.
- Never load password hashes, TOTP secrets, recovery codes, or other credentials for display/list queries. For 2FA display, select only `enabledAt`.
- Keep authorization, tenant scope, filtering, ordering, and bounded pagination server-side regardless of selection. Omitted fields are not authorization.
- Use purpose-specific typed reads for materially different projections; avoid generic selection reflection that weakens type or tenant safeguards.
- Follow `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` → “GraphQL field selection and data efficiency”.
